00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.870
There are three of them on the FTP site.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:48.080
We talked about the one using EntrezDirect
the last time that I gave webinar.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.940
This time we are using BLAST.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:56.560
The idea is you can use them to teach undergraduate
courses that involve bioinformatics and BLAST

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.660
as a starting point for making your own examples.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.720
Here's what we will do today.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.740
We will talk a little bit about BLAST.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.170
A few tips about BLAST that everybody should
know.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:15.540
A little evangelism about things that we in
blast-help think people should know.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.490
Talk about some related alignment services.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.520
Then we will go through the examples.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.520
Five different ones.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.969
One of them is two parts.

00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:28.659
One will be identifying bacteria using the
16S rRNA.

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:31.530
Designing primers for a gene product.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.860
We will make some trees using some of the
add-ons to BLAST.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.689
We will do one that shows the phylogeny of
the apes using mitchondrialgenomes.

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:46.219
One that shows the protein evolution where
there are a number of paralogs of creatine

00:01:46.219 --> 00:01:50.130
kinases.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.649
Annotate an unannotated bacterial contig.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:55.090
And then map a protein.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:02:00.100
In fact the same one we got from the bacteria
contig, onto a conserved domain, and a 3D

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.100
structure.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.979
So a couple of things.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:04.889
What is BLAST?

00:02:04.889 --> 00:02:08.250
This is the most widely used sequence similarity
tool in the world.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.360
It finds high-scoring local alignments between
two sequence.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:18.190
It includes a model of score distributions
for random local alignments, which means it

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.890
can provide statistical significance for alignments.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:26.819
The main statistic is the Expect value, the
number of hits with the same or better score

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:28.709
than expected by chance.

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:32.700
In practice, it doesn't come up that much
in people's everyday searches.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.379
We will talk about that in a moment.

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:38.209
A reminder of what BLAST tells you about.

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:42.620
It tells you about non-chance similarities
between biological sequences.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.129
If they're not due to chance, then they are
due to something else.

00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:49.920
Homology, is the classic thing that BLAST
is about.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.030
These are related because they evolved from
the common ancestors.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:03:00.030
But in practice, a lot of times BLAST is used
simply to identify something.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.650
We'll talk more about that in a moment.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:06.409
In order to use BLAST you have to have a nucleotide
or protein sequence.

00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:11.079
These are the three related things blast does.

00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:13.010
You can answer the following questions.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.689
What is my sequence related to and what does
it do?

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:20.750
This is about homology or conserved domains.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:21.980
Is it already in the database?

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:22.980
Identification.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:31.530
Find the matching sequence or you could find
the organism that sequence probably belong

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:32.530
to.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:37.140
And the last thing is about assembly or annotation
issues.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.430
Where is it located?

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.560
How is it organized?

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.380
What kind of things are on my sequence?

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.620
The evangelism part I wanted to mention.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:54.120
This has to do with the size of the BLAST
databases.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.939
They are quite large.

00:03:55.939 --> 00:03:59.219
The default ones are enormous.

00:03:59.219 --> 00:04:02.290
In general the bigger the search space the
longer the search takes.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:06.159
I will add the more likely it is to fail.

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:12.060
And the expect value for a particular hit
increases linearly with the size of the database.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.389
That is kind of a cryptic statement.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:21.160
It means this, you're much more likely to
be able to get and interpret your results

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.470
if you search the smallest database that is
likely to contain the target of interest.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.160
That is one of the things I want to make clear.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.540
Most searches are against the default databases.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.600
We will look at some of these.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.940
Refseq protein, the model organisms database.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:42.730
The 16S rRNA database.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.890
Always, I can't think of an example when he
wouldn't want to do this, use an organism

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:51.660
or an Entrez query to limit to reduce the
database size.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:57.750
You can adjust the expect value cutoff to
limit to significant matches.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:02.070
Here a couple more words about the program.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:06.920
The nucleotide search programs by default
use megablast.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:14.800
There will be times when you may need to make
the more that search more sensitive.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.470
You can change the nucleotide search program.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.660
For protein searches you can do a straight
BLASTP.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:28.650
You can also translate and we will do a blastx
search to look at an unannotated nucleotide

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:29.650
sequence.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.930
We will do a conserved domains search.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:39.100
Which tells us about functional elements or
similar kinds of structures.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.970
This has a greater look back time.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:46.030
It tells you more about the structure function
of a protein than ordinary BLAST.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.880
That runs every time you run a BLASTP search.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.010
We will use some other alignment services.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:54.240
They are not BLAST they are related.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.400
Primer blast.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.630
Which will help you design primers that are
specific.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:06.950
MOLE-BLAST is a demonstration program but
we will use it with the 16S as a good thing

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:12.470
for clustering and finding out what the sources
are, and COBALT our protein multiple alignment

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.470
tool.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:14.480
How do you get to BLAST?

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.970
Go to the BLAST homepage.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.340
Access to all programs.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:23.880
Any of the main programs you can change what
kind of search are doing right on the page.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:29.940
The other tools listed at the bottom are the
ones we will use today.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:36.370
MOLE-BLAST, primer blast, and multiple alignment.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.310
Here the things will do.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.590
Identify uncultured bacterial 16S sequences.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:47.350
Design primers to amplify the coding region
of a gene the human myeloperoxidase, MPO,

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:50.330
transcript and exon 10 of the gene.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:58.750
Make phylogenetic trees based on the ape mitochondrial
genome sequences, including human.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.870
Also tetrapod vertebrate creatine kinase genes.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:09.090
Tetrapods are creatures with 4 legs like us,
we call two of them arms.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.580
Annotate coding regions on a prokaryotic genomic
sequence metagenome.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.940
Find conserved domains in that uncharaterized
protein.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:22.570
What I will do for each search is set it up
and go ahead and show you what happens when

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:24.300
we do it.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:33.630
The first one will be identifying these 20
uncultured 16S rRNA's in the database.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:39.730
We can use our 16S rRNA type strains database
on the BLAST page, get a distant tree of the

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:40.730
results.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:41.730
Manipulate those things.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.030
And we can look at MOLE-BLAST.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.660
Let's set that up.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.600
I will go to the website.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.820
What I have up here is a cheat sheet for me.

