00:19:11.659 --> 00:19:17.880
Then we can manipulate the treeviewer display
to get a particular kind of tree out of this.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:19.539
Let's go to website again.

00:19:19.539 --> 00:19:24.519
what I want to do is to get a particular sequence
as a query.

00:19:24.519 --> 00:19:34.879
What I have done is chosen what a lot of taxonomists
call and an outgroup sequence.

00:19:34.879 --> 00:19:41.499
This is from a primate that is more distanly
related to apes.

00:19:41.499 --> 00:19:42.840
Like humans.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:46.580
This is a ring tailed lemur.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:53.669
To set this up I would choose run BLAST here.

00:19:53.669 --> 00:19:55.150
Change my database.

00:19:55.150 --> 00:20:01.390
To RefSEq Genomic . Enter my organism name
here.

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:07.809
Which is hominoidea, which is a super family
that contains humans and other apes.

00:20:07.809 --> 00:20:15.789
I will also put an Entrez query limit in here,
mitochondrion[filter], that would limit to

00:20:15.789 --> 00:20:17.970
mitochondrial sequences.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:22.140
Sometimes there are mitochondrial sequences
within the nuclear genome.

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:28.440
This is the mitochondrial genome of the lemur
up here, the query NC_004025.1

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:32.289
I will pick one of the more sensitive algorithm.

00:20:32.289 --> 00:20:34.259
Discontiguous mega blast.

00:20:34.259 --> 00:20:39.799
You can adjust the algorithm parameters down
here such as the E value.

00:20:39.799 --> 00:20:42.100
I want to make it so things are stringent.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:44.169
I can put in 1e-64.

00:20:44.169 --> 00:20:49.090
Then there would be no chance that these are
random alignments.

00:20:49.090 --> 00:20:52.690
These will be things that are distinctly significant.

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:58.879
I will retrieve that result for you.

00:20:58.879 --> 00:21:10.960
Now I have hits to several of the great apes
here.

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:20.460
You might recognize the names, chimpanzees,
gorillas, siamang and the gibbons.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:29.130
I can use the Distance tree of results link
to get the tree.

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:31.020
This is an interesting display.

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:36.629
Something more useful may be to change the
way this is laid out.

00:21:36.629 --> 00:21:50.500
Make it a circular tree.

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:54.649
Here are the humans you will notice there
are extinct humans.

00:21:54.649 --> 00:21:56.419
Hominims.

00:21:56.419 --> 00:22:03.149
The closest relatives to us are the chimpanzee
and bonobo.

00:22:03.149 --> 00:22:05.120
Here are the other great apes in the same
family.

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:11.820
The family hominidae is here.

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:14.460
These are the gibbons here.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:21.749
Here is our outgroup, the lemur.

00:22:21.749 --> 00:22:24.500
This is an example that you can run yourself.

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:27.129
This is homology search using the creatine
kinases.

00:22:27.129 --> 00:22:31.879
It is the same principle as the one we just
did, except that this time we'll be making

00:22:31.879 --> 00:22:34.169
a protein tree.

00:22:34.169 --> 00:22:38.609
Because there are numerous paralogs in here
you will have multiple homologues in the same

00:22:38.609 --> 00:22:41.659
species (paralogs).

00:22:41.659 --> 00:22:45.760
The extension of this is you can run COBALT
directly from the BLAST result to generate

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:47.159
your tree.

00:22:47.159 --> 00:22:50.610
It is exactly the same procedure.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:55.070
You can use the links in the handout to get
there if you want to.

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:59.769
We will go ahead and do this one and we will
need to wrap up.

00:22:59.769 --> 00:23:03.030
This is identifying a coding region on a genomic
sequence.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:09.049
We're going to use a sequence from a marine
metagenome, it is unannotated.

00:23:09.049 --> 00:23:11.230
Search against a small database called Landmark.

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:15.570
It will give us some good matches.

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:27.999
It contains representatives from all groups
of organisms, bacteria, archaea, plants, animals.

00:23:27.999 --> 00:23:32.240
Let's go to the website.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:35.240
Start with a contig from this prokaryotic,
marine metagenome.

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:46.059
This 
has no annotation associated with.

00:23:46.059 --> 00:23:50.239
You can run BLAST with it.

00:23:50.239 --> 00:23:52.259
And what we will do is change this up.

00:23:52.259 --> 00:23:55.460
We will go to a translating BLAST search.

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.129
This is blastx.

00:23:58.129 --> 00:24:00.210
Use the bacteria and Archaea genetic code.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:05.250
And we will use the Landmark Model Organisms
database.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:13.470
You can leave it like this with bacteria and
archea.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.030
Or I can do the whole thing and leave it wide
open.

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:19.200
I think the one I showed you was leaving the
whole thing wide open.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:23.340
I did change a couple of parameters here.

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:27.289
The E value threshold was set to a more significant
value.

00:24:27.289 --> 00:24:30.049
1e-6, which I suggest you always do.

00:24:30.049 --> 00:24:35.320
I set the word size from the default of 6
to 3 to make it a more sensitive search.

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:46.169
Let's take a quick look at those results.

00:24:46.169 --> 00:24:47.480
Here is our query sequence.

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:54.029
Each one of these matches is to a protein
from one of the organism's proteomes in the

00:24:54.029 --> 00:24:55.029
database.

00:24:55.029 --> 00:24:58.690
For example here is a hef nuclease.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:24:59.720
ANother helicase.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:01.929
These are all related proteins.

00:25:01.929 --> 00:25:06.210
Here is a DNA topoisomerase I.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:14.559
The other titles in this position are all
DNA topoisomerase I. Here is a tRNA ligase

00:25:14.559 --> 00:25:19.649
for alanine.

00:25:19.649 --> 00:25:22.710
Annotated that way from various organisms.

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:26.950
The homology extends across into the plants.

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:34.450
You see evidence for six protein coding genes.

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:45.970
The best hit is to this Archaeal sequences.

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:48.580
Down here in the Alignments you have the coding
region.

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:54.070
We've matched a certain part of the query
sequence, which is nucleotide sequence coordinates.

00:25:54.070 --> 00:25:58.480
The query ends here and notice it is on the
opposite strand, frame minus 3.

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:02.249
Notice what we don't have are the coordinates
of the actual open reading frame.

00:26:02.249 --> 00:26:09.309
An extension of this example is to use that
with our ORFfinder tool, which is in the document,

00:26:09.309 --> 00:26:10.929
to get the complete open reading frame.

00:26:10.929 --> 00:26:16.499
In fact if you do that, you will find that
you get a complete open reading that you can

00:26:16.499 --> 00:26:22.139
use in the next example, which is a BLAST
search against PDB where you can find a structure

00:26:22.139 --> 00:26:24.070
module and a conserved domain.

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:26.889
We've run over time.

00:26:26.889 --> 00:26:31.549
I will stop here and point you to the document
itself.

00:26:31.549 --> 00:26:34.529
You can take a look at the additional examples
that are in there.

00:26:34.529 --> 00:26:38.770
Hopefully you will find them useful in any
teaching that you have to do using BLAST.

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:45.710
There are five or probably six if you count
the two trees is two different examples.

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:49.619
Examples that you can adapt easily to teaching.

00:26:49.619 --> 00:26:54.360
I think they would be useful for developing
teaching exercises for undergraduate courses.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:27:00.679
The last slide has links to some URLs that
are relevant to the material we talked about

00:27:00.679 --> 00:27:01.679
today.

00:27:01.679 --> 00:27:03.519
And material in the handout.

00:27:03.519 --> 00:27:07.769
The Teaching by Example, the five different
ones, are available in our Factsheets directory.

00:27:07.769 --> 00:27:10.789
ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pub/factsheets/
Let's take a few minutes for questions.

00:27:10.789 --> 00:27:11.789
Do we have any?

00:27:11.789 --> 00:27:16.419
[Wayne Matten speaking] So one question was,
for the ape mitochondria search, why did not

00:27:16.419 --> 00:27:18.379
lemur show up in the results?

00:27:18.379 --> 00:27:23.590
[Peter Cooper speaking] The reason lemurs
did not show up because I limited the database

00:27:23.590 --> 00:27:26.570
by using an organism query.

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:34.179
And I limited it to the superfamily Hominoidia,
which in our taxonomy database maps to the

00:27:34.179 --> 00:27:35.549
commone word ape.

00:27:35.549 --> 00:27:42.739
That contains the great apes which are the
humans, chimps, and gorillas.

00:27:42.739 --> 00:27:44.239
And orangutans and gibbons.

00:27:44.239 --> 00:27:51.710
The lemur is a primate but it is not part
of that superfamily.

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:55.109
The query sequence does not show up in our
results.

00:27:55.109 --> 00:28:00.029
Had we used primates, we would have gotten
the lemur sequence in our results.

00:28:00.029 --> 00:28:05.330
[Wayne] One more, a general question, What
is that difference between COBALT and other

00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:07.129
multiple sequence aligners like ClustaW?

00:28:07.129 --> 00:28:11.919
]Peter] Okay so what's the difference between
COBALT and multiple sequence alignment tools

00:28:11.919 --> 00:28:13.669
like ClustalW.

00:28:13.669 --> 00:28:19.700
The difference is largely what sort of, a
trick that a lot of multiple alignment tools

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:26.570
use is an initial guide tree to cluster the
sequences before they proceed with the multiple

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:27.570
alignment.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:31.450
It reduces amount of computation time required.

00:28:31.450 --> 00:28:34.059
COBALT uses a constraint.

00:28:34.059 --> 00:28:40.549
The constraint is matches to the conserved
domains in the protein.

00:28:40.549 --> 00:28:50.849
The constraint we use is the conserved domains
in the proteins themselves to begin to make

00:28:50.849 --> 00:28:53.169
a guide tree to generate that alignment.

00:28:53.169 --> 00:28:59.590
There is probably a more technical explanation
in the COBALT paper.

00:28:59.590 --> 00:29:03.450
They all have their merits and drawbacks.

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:04.970
Multiple alignment tools.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:08.470
If there are no other questions, Wayne says
no.

